WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.767
-Our next guest is a Grammy
Award-winning country music

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:05.100
star. Her cookbook, here it is
right there.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.567
"Y'all Eat Yet? Welcome to
the Pretty Bitchin' Kitchen"

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:08.868
is available now.
Please welcome to the show

00:00:08.868 --> 00:00:10.634
our friend Miranda Lambert,
everybody.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:11.834
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:19.100
♪♪

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:26.367
♪♪

00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.667
Welcome to the show.

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:29.934
You've been here three times
performing for us.

00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:31.300
It's the first time
we get to chat.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.634
I'm so happy to be
doing this with you.

00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:35.467
It's a wonderful book,
not just recipes.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.400
Also a great many stories,
it feels like.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.501
Is that what made it fun

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:41.334
to not just write
a conventional cookbook,

00:00:41.334 --> 00:00:43.601
but one that you got
to tell about yourself

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:44.801
and the people in your life?

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:46.133
-Yeah, I've never
written a book.

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:47.400
I mean, I'm a songwriter,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.300
so it didn't seem
that hard till I started.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.767
I was like, "Oh, man, this is a
totally different ballgame."

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.133
-Really? Because
you don't have to --

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.667
You never have to rhyme
in a cookbook.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.234
So that seems like
it should be easier.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:59.734
-It's not three minutes long.
It's like, yeah, it was --

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:03.334
It was a journey. We've been
working on it since since 2020.

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:07.467
So my co-writer Holly Gleason,
like, made sense of my stories

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.968
and my redneck way
of telling them.

00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:12.200
So that's -- she made it
all make sense.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.300
-But some wonderful characters

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.901
who find their way
into this cookbook.

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:18.234
Your mother, Bev -- great mom
name, Bev, let me say.

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:20.267
-Yeah.
-Grandmother Wanda.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.133
Maybe even better.
-Yep.

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.868
-You also talk about sort of
a group of friends,

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:26.667
women friends of your life
that you referred to as

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.334
as the Ya-Yas?
Am I saying that right?

00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:29.734
-Yeah.
So my grandmother had

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:32.100
this big group of girlfriends
that we called the Ya-Yas.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.267
They were generation
one, we would call them.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.934
And my mom and her best friends
were like my aunts, basically.

00:01:36.934 --> 00:01:40.734
They raised me.
So it's basically just stories,

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.434
memories, like, growing up

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:44.667
with a group of strong women
around me like that.

00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:46.367
It really prepared me
for the world.

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:49.300
And, like, I've been in the
music industry for 20 years,

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.901
so I feel like
without their wisdom,

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:52.701
I don't know
if I could have made it.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:54.200
But this is like --
it's more stories

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.634
I've never shared with anybody.

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:58.267
I mean, I'm really honest
in my songs and my records,

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:00.167
but I've never put some of these

00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:02.200
really personal stories
and memories out there.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.234
And there's just recipes
to go with it.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:06.868
And the recipes are like
very approachable recipes.

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:09.601
They use like butter
and Velveeta and stuff.

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:12.400
[ Laughter ]
-Anyone can do it.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.367
-There was a very novel recipe
for how you used to --

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:17.567
I guess you would
do this growing up --

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:20.734
how you would make omelets
for camping trips.

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:22.868
-Yeah.
-This was tricky.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:24.667
Obviously, when you think
about making an omelet

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:26.300
on a camping trip, you're like,
"That'll never work.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:27.434
You got to bring
too much stuff."

00:02:27.434 --> 00:02:28.868
-Right. And when you explain it,

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:29.968
it doesn't sound
super glamorous.

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:31.400
But we all go
on glamping trips.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.234
All these girls in this book,
we all had Airstreams,

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:35.734
so we would go like once
or twice a year

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:37.234
on an all-girls camping trip.

00:02:37.234 --> 00:02:39.467
And we like to make it easy so
we can drink more and hang out.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:40.734
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.734 --> 00:02:43.434
The Ya-Yas, let me say,
I would love membership.

00:02:43.434 --> 00:02:46.334
-Yes, you're in.
-It sounds like a lot of fun

00:02:46.334 --> 00:02:47.501
to hang out with the Ya-Yas.
-You're in.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:48.868
You're absolutely in.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.567
And so we made up this way
of doing omelets in a bag.

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:54.100
I think it was my dad's idea
actually

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.767
because we grew up
tent camping, you know,

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.100
but now we're like, "No,
we don't sleep on the ground.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.767
We sleep in Airstreams."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:00.367
But you put all the fixings

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:02.901
for whatever omelet you want
in there, like ham and cheese,

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:05.367
onion, whatever.
-In a bag, in a Ziploc bag?

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:07.200
-You go ahead and you
whip it all up, put it in a bag,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.334
and then you drop it
in hot water and it cooks.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:12.601
Or you can bring your,
you know, your glamorous --

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.968
-So, wait. You have a Ziploc bag
full of just the stuff --

00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:17.701
-It sounds disgusting.
We shouldn't say it out loud.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.334
-So you put it in a boiling...

00:03:19.334 --> 00:03:22.167
-Can do that or you
can just like -- These girls

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.167
are, like, they go hard.

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:26.467
They, like, show up anywhere
with their own electric skillet

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.934
just in case anybody gets --
they need to whip something up.

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:30.434
-Right.
-And so we love a party,

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:33.033
if you can't tell.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.067
-That's a whole nother
level of party if you're like,

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:37.601
"I want to make sure
I got a skillet just in case."

00:03:37.601 --> 00:03:38.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.400
Your favorite
cocktail is a bluebird?

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.701
What is a bluebird?

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:43.601
-A bluebird --
You can make it with tequila.

00:03:43.601 --> 00:03:45.734
You can make it with bourbon.
You can make it with Tito's.

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:48.501
I usually do tequila or Tito's,

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:51.968
and it is a muddled blueberries
and mint and some soda

00:03:51.968 --> 00:03:53.667
and a little bit
of simple syrup.

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:55.767
And it's supposed to be
like two ounces of whatever.

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:58.434
But I'm like, "Mm..."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.434 --> 00:03:59.801
-You're a friendly pour?

00:03:59.801 --> 00:04:01.667
-Yeah, I just do
like a six count.

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:03.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:05.934
-Now, sometimes muddling like,
I will say,

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.834
if I'm behind in line
behind someone at a bar

00:04:08.834 --> 00:04:10.067
and I hear them
ordering a drink

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:11.601
where muddling needs
to take place,

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:13.400
I'm pretty upset, you know.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.934
But I would imagine you probably
after a couple of these,

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:17.934
you're probably not putting too
much time into the muddling.

00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:19.934
-Yeah, I think the first two,
you should really care.

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:21.434
[ Laughter ]
And then after that,

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:23.467
you're like, how about just
the soda and the tequila?

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.434
That'd be great.

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:27.501
-Have any of the Ya-Yas
read the book?

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.501
Are they excited about?
-They're so excited.

00:04:29.501 --> 00:04:31.767
We're going to do a little trip
we've been talking about.

00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:33.167
We're all from Texas.
So we were like,

00:04:33.167 --> 00:04:34.634
"Where have we not been
together?"

00:04:34.634 --> 00:04:36.367
We want to go
maybe to like Sedona

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:38.701
and have a celebration, just
somewhere different, you know?

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:40.667
But they're so excited.
It's really cool.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:44.367
I mean, we were just talking.
During 2020, we would get on --

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:47.033
We started this thing we called
the Chopped Cook-Off

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:49.801
where it was just an excuse
to drink together on FaceTime

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.300
for like three hours.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.100
But our husbands would
all pick an ingredient

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.334
and we would have to make
a random dish

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:59.400
out of the five ingredients.
But you couldn't Google.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.534
That was the rule.
It was really hard.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:02.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:04.634
Like one of them
was like chili powder.

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:07.000
And you're like, I don't know
how much to use of whatever,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.100
so that's what even
started this whole thing.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.834
It's like we started
talking about our memories

00:05:10.834 --> 00:05:13.501
and how our sisterhood
really has formed

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:15.534
all of our adult lives,
you know?

00:05:15.534 --> 00:05:16.801
I mean, these ladies raised me

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:19.067
and my grandma's friends
raised them.

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:20.901
So it felt like -- I feel like

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:22.300
there's a lot of people
out there

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.901
that have that circle that lifts
them up and celebrates them,

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:27.067
that's with you through
the hard times.

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:28.601
And that's basically
what this book is about.

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:29.734
-You mentioned you're
from Texas.

00:05:29.734 --> 00:05:32.234
Your husband, former NYPD?
-Yeah.

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:33.801
-You met him here in New York?
-Yeah.

00:05:33.801 --> 00:05:35.167
-You guys lived here
for a little bit.

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.467
Are you Nashville now?
-Yeah, we're Nashville.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:39.033
We're up here a lot.
We spend a lot of time up here.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:40.367
We have tons of family.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.567
-How is he adjusting, though,
to the Nashville life?

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:44.701
-He loves it.
I mean, he's, you know,

00:05:44.701 --> 00:05:47.067
it took him a minute
to get used to the slower pace.

00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.968
But Nashville is city enough
for, like, a city person

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:51.767
and country enough
for a country person.

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:54.067
So it's a good medium.
But I love New York.

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:56.501
I had never kind of
got lost in the city

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:59.200
or spent time here
besides working until I met him.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.567
And we had an apartment
in SoHo for six months.

00:06:01.567 --> 00:06:05.834
And I just like immersed myself
in NYC and I loved it so much.

00:06:05.834 --> 00:06:08.033
-Who's the better chef, you
or your husband?

00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:10.133
-100% him.
-But you wrote a cookbook.

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:12.834
-I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:15.234
He says that every day.
He's like, "Cool, cool, bro."

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:17.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:17.067 --> 00:06:19.367
He's the cook of our family.

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:21.901
So I told him. I was like,
five years from now,

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:23.200
I'm calling it, like,
we'll be up here

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.367
and I'll just be sitting
in the dressing room

00:06:24.367 --> 00:06:25.701
drinking like he is

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:27.868
and he'll be doing the work,
promoting his cookbook.

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:28.901
-Well, I'll tell you this.

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:29.934
Let him know that
if he writes a cookbook,

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:31.100
I promise he can come
on here to talk.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.434
-He's here.
-Okay, good. That's a promise.

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.734
That is 100% a promise.
[ Applause ]

00:06:35.734 --> 00:06:37.734
You've got the ACMs coming up.

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:39.100
You've got a lot of nominations.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.367
When you go to one of these
country music award shows,

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:43.868
are you someone
who gets nervous now?

00:06:43.868 --> 00:06:45.100
-Yeah.
I mean, national television

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.300
is always a little nerve-racking
no matter what.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.567
But I love it.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:50.200
You know, this will be
my 18th year at the ACMs.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.300
-That's so exciting.
-That's crazy.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.133
And Nashville,
the country music community

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:57.567
is so supportive and friendly.
And like, people always ask me

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:59.100
like, "Is everybody
really that nice?"

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.534
I'm like,
"Yeah, we kind of are."

00:07:00.534 --> 00:07:02.200
You know, it's like
we're friends.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.033
Friendly competition, but
everybody deserves to be there.

00:07:05.033 --> 00:07:07.634
-It seems like it's very hard
to have a long career

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:08.734
in Nashville
where you don't work

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:10.067
with almost everybody
in the business

00:07:10.067 --> 00:07:11.667
or someone in their band,

00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:13.667
and it seems
very lovely that way.

00:07:13.667 --> 00:07:15.901
-It's definitely
a community and a family.

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:19.000
And I mean, I've been, you know,
doing this for 20 years

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.267
and I'm still getting nominated
and get to go see my friends.

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:23.033
And it's like,
it's such a good feeling.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:25.567
It makes me
want to work even harder.

00:07:25.567 --> 00:07:27.501
-It's also cool when you
do things you've never done

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:29.367
before after a career
this impressive.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:30.968
You've got a residency
in Las Vegas.

00:07:30.968 --> 00:07:32.901
How are you enjoying
your Las Vegas life so far?

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:35.200
-I love it.
It was very different.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.734
I mean, it kind of freaked me
out the first time.

00:07:36.734 --> 00:07:39.767
I, like, went and did a show

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:41.067
and there was no bus there,
you know?

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:43.267
I mean, I've lived
on a bus forever.

00:07:43.267 --> 00:07:44.934
And I was really excited,
though,

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:47.501
because we get to do
this crazy production

00:07:47.501 --> 00:07:49.367
we've never gotten
to take on the road.

00:07:49.367 --> 00:07:50.601
When you don't have to load it
into trucks

00:07:50.601 --> 00:07:51.701
every night,
it's a lot easier.

00:07:51.701 --> 00:07:55.000
I'm like, "More fire!
More confetti!"

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.067
So, like, I just got to take
everything up a notch.

00:07:58.067 --> 00:08:00.067
And being a Texan, like,

00:08:00.067 --> 00:08:03.567
there's no such thing as too
much in Vegas of anything.

00:08:03.567 --> 00:08:05.467
-Yeah.
-Especially rhinestones.

00:08:05.467 --> 00:08:07.100
So I'm like -- I took it --

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.200
I took it all the way
to the next level.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.167
-No one will leave
your show in Vegas and go,

00:08:11.167 --> 00:08:12.567
"I thought there'd be
more rhinestones."

00:08:12.567 --> 00:08:14.767
Is that what you're saying?
-Nope. They will not.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:16.601
-You also have a wonderful --
I want to get this right,

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:18.467
your charity
MuttNation Foundation.

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:19.634
-Yeah.
-Can you tell us a little bit

00:08:19.634 --> 00:08:21.100
about what you're doing
with MuttNation?

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:22.467
Which is a fantastic name
for a charity, by the way.

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:23.634
-Yes. Thank you.

00:08:23.634 --> 00:08:25.434
I started it with my mom
and we do a lot together.

00:08:25.434 --> 00:08:27.100
-Bev.
-Bev. Bev Lambert.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.400
Beverly June Lambert.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.767
-Beverly June.
It keeps getting better.

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.167
-It gets better.
-You can't beat Bev Lambert.

00:08:32.167 --> 00:08:33.868
There's a June in the middle.
Well, that's better.

00:08:33.868 --> 00:08:35.300
[ Laughter ]

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:38.133
-We started it in 2009
just to basically --

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:41.467
I had started
to get a platform in my career

00:08:41.467 --> 00:08:43.234
where it's time
to pick a charity

00:08:43.234 --> 00:08:46.501
that you really pour into.
And I love animals.

00:08:46.501 --> 00:08:47.901
And I felt like
I didn't want to just give

00:08:47.901 --> 00:08:49.133
to certain animal charities.

00:08:49.133 --> 00:08:51.834
I wanted to kind of --
We wanted to hand-pick.

00:08:51.834 --> 00:08:54.467
And so we started
our own, MuttNation Foundation,

00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:57.734
and we just basically
raise awareness for shelter dogs

00:08:57.734 --> 00:09:00.367
and we lift up shelters,
shelters that need help.

